: string): MergeResult {\n  // 1. Get current state, source state, and base state\n  const current = this.branchState.get(this.currentBranch)!;\n  const source = this.branchState.get(sourceBranch)!;\n  const base = this.branchBase.get(sourceBranch)!;\n  \n  // 2. Collect all document IDs involved\n  const allDocIds = new Set<string>();\n  for (const key of current.keys()) allDocIds.add(key);\n  for (const key of source.keys()) allDocIds.add(key);\n  for (const key of base.keys()) allDocIds.add(key);\n  \n  const merged: Record<string, object> = {};\n  const conflicts: Conflict[] = [];\n  let hasConflicts = false;\n  \n  // 3. For each document, perform three-way merge\n  for (const docId of allDocIds) {\n    const baseVersionId = base.get(docId);\n    const ourVersionId = current.get(docId);\n    const theirVersionId = source.get(docId);\n    \n    // Determine what changed\n    // ... (the logic described above)\n    \n    const result = this.mergeDocument(docId, baseVersionId, ourVersionId, theirVersionId, conflicts);\n    if (result !== undefined) {\n      merged[docId] = result.content;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // 4. If no conflicts, apply\n  if (!hasConflicts) {\n    // Apply merge to current branch\n    for (const [docId, versionId] of ...) {\n      // Create new versions and update branch state\n    }\n    return { merged, conflicts: [], applied: true };\n  }\n  \n  return { merged, conflicts, applied: false };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, creating new versions for merged docs... When we apply a merge, should we create new versions for the documents? I think yes - to capture the merge result.\n\nOK let me just write the code now. I'll create a clean implementation.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n1. A document existed on current branch but not on source or base (created after fork on current)\n   -> It's a new doc on current branch, just include it in merged result\n2. A document exists on all three but no changes -> skip it\n3. Merging a branch into itself -> should result in no changes\n\nActually for edge case 3, merging a branch into itself could happen if `merge('main')` while on 'main'. That should just be a no-op or throw an error. Let me check... the spec says nothing about this. I'll make it a no-op (applied: true, empty merged).\n\nWait, re-reading the spec: \"A merge with no changes on either side is a no-op (fast-forward).\" So a no-change merge is fine.\n\nOK let me now just write the code.\n\nHere's my plan for the implementation:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  types.ts    - Type definitions (Version, MergeResult, Conflict)\n  utils.ts    - Utility functions (deepClone, deepEqual, isPlainObject)\n  merge.ts    - Three-way merge logic\n  index.ts    - DocumentStore class\n```\n\nLet me write each file.\n\n### src/types.ts\nStraightforward type definitions.\n\n### src/utils.ts\n- `deepClone(obj)` -> deep clone using JSON.parse+stringify\n- `deepEqual(a, b)` -> deep equality\n- `isPlainObject(obj)` -> check if it's a plain object (not null, not array, typeof === 'object')\n- `generateVersionId(counter)` -> generate unique version ID\n\n### src/merge.ts\n- `threeWayMerge(base, ours, theirs, docId, path)` -> { content, conflicts }\n\n### src/index.ts\n- DocumentStore class with all the methods\n\nLet me write it all out now.\n\nFor version IDs, I'll use `v${counter}` where counter is an incrementing global counter.\n\n```typescript\nprivate nextVersionId(): string {\n  return `v${++this.versionCounter}`;\n}\n```\n\nFor timestamps, I'll use `Date.now()`.\n\nLet me also think about `getHistory` and `getVersion`:\n\n`getHistory(id)` should return all versions of a document across all branches, or just on the current branch? The spec says \"Returns versions in chronological order (oldest first).\" I think it should return all versions of the document regardless of branch, since versions are stored globally.\n\nBut that might be weird for cross-branch visibility. Let me re-read: \"Version History: Every create and update generates a new version.\" So any create or update on any branch creates a version. `getHistory` returns the history. I think it should return all versions of the document.\n\nLet me look at the test expectations again... The spec doesn't give specific test cases. Let me just make `getHistory` return all versions of the document from the global version store, and `getVersion` fetch from there too.\n\nAlright, let me now write the code.\n\nFor the document-level merge, I need to think about what happens when:\n1. Document is in base and ours, but not in source (they deleted it)\n2. Document is in base and source, but not in ours (we deleted it)\n3. Document is not in base, but in both ours and source (both created it)\n4. Document is not in base, but only in ours (we created it)\n5. Document is not in base, but only in source (they created it)\n6. Document is in all three\n\nLet me track \"version ID\" of null for deleted, and undefined for not-in-base.\n\nActually, let me simplify. For the branchState maps:\n- Not present (no entry) = document was never created on this branch\n- null value = document was deleted on this branch\n- versionId string = document exists with that version\n\nFor the base map (fork point):\n- Not present = document didn't exist at fork point\n- null = document was deleted at fork point? (unlikely but possible if forked from a branch that deleted the doc)\n- versionId string = document existed with that version\n\nWait, can a branch be created that has deleted documents? I don't think so, because the base would just... hmm, the state at fork point includes deleted docs? I think the base should only track what existed at fork point. If a doc was deleted before the fork, it's just not in the base state.\n\nLet me think about this differently. When branch B is created from branch A:\n- B starts with the same documents as A\n- For each doc in A, B has the same version ID (or null if deleted)\n- B's base = a copy of B's state at creation\n\nSo yes, the base can have