n main, there's no conflict.\n\nAnd if I created a doc on the branch, and someone else created the same doc on main (independently), merging should detect a conflict.\n\nOK, I think tracking with null is necessary. Let me keep the null entries.\n\nBut for the base state, I'll only include non-null entries because the base represents \"what existed at fork point\", and deleted docs are more of a \"state\" thing.\n\nActually, let me simplify even further. I'll track the state differently:\n\nFor each branch:\n- `documents`: Map<docId, versionId> - only exists for documents that currently exist on this branch\n- `deleted`: Set<docId> - documents that were deleted on this branch\n\nNo wait, that gets complicated too because we need to know if a doc was recently deleted vs never existed.\n\nLet me just use a simpler mental model:\n\nThe branch state is `Map<docId, string>` where the string is the HEAD version ID. If a document is deleted, it's REMOVED from the map entirely. To detect \"modify vs delete\" conflicts during merge, when the base has a doc that's not in the source branch's documents, we know it was deleted.\n\nBut how do we distinguish \"deleted\" from \"never existed on this branch\"? We use the base: if the base has a doc, it existed at fork time. If it doesn't exist in the current branch state now, it was either never created or was deleted. But the base tells us it existed at fork time, so it must have been deleted.\n\nHmm, this makes assumptions about the branch DAG. If we branch from main, then main creates a new doc, and later we merge main into our branch... actually, that's not how branching works in this system. Changes on main after the fork aren't visible on our branch until we merge.\n\nOK I think the simplest approach works: branch state has only currently-existing documents. When we merge, we look at (base, ours, theirs) where:\n- ours = current branch state (Map<docId, versionId>)\n- base = source branch's base (Map<docId, versionId>) - the state at fork time\n- theirs = source branch state (Map<docId, versionId>)\n\nFor a doc in base but not in theirs: they deleted it.\nFor a doc in base but not in ours: we deleted it.\nFor a doc not in base but in ours: we created it.\nFor a doc not in base but in theirs: they created it.\nFor a doc in all three: both may have modified it.\n\nWait, but what about a doc created on main, then we create a branch, then delete on the branch? The doc is in base (it was in main at fork time) and not in ours (we deleted it). When merging back to main, this means \"we deleted it, they didn't change it\" -> take our deletion. That's correct - it gets deleted from main.\n\nBut in the merged result, do we track \"deleted\" documents? The merged field is `Record<string, object>` - it only has documentId -> content for existing documents. Deleted docs just don't appear in the merged result. That seems right.\n\nOK, let me now think about the merge result for deleted docs. If both sides deleted a doc, it just doesn't appear in the merged result. If only one side deleted it (and the other didn't change it), it doesn't appear. If one side deleted and the other modified, that's a conflict, and the merged result should include the \"ours\" version.\n\nWait, the spec says: \"the merged field always contains the merge result (with conflicts resolved in favor of 'ours' for the purpose of showing what it would look like).\"\n\nSo even in the face of conflicts, we produce a merged result with conflicts resolved in favor of ours. For a modify-vs-delete conflict, if we deleted it but they modified it, the merged result wouldn't include it (since we deleted it). If they deleted it but we modified it, it would include our modified version.\n\nOK, I think I have enough understanding. Let me write the code now.\n\nLet me think about one more thing: the applied state. When there are no conflicts and we apply the merge:\n\n1. For each document that was merged (potentially with content from both sides):\n   - Create a new version with the merged content\n   - Update the current branch's state to point to this new version\n   \n2. For new documents from the source branch:\n   - Create versions for them\n   - Add them to the current branch\n   \n3. For deleted documents:\n   - Remove them from the current branch (or mark as deleted)\n\nLet me also think about versioning for the merged documents. When we auto-apply a merge, we want the merged content to be the new state. Should we create a new version for this? I think yes, to keep a clear history. The merged content is a new version derived from the merge.\n\nBut what about documents that weren't changed during the merge? They should remain pointing to the same version.\n\nOK, I'll create new versions only for documents whose content actually changed as a result of the merge.\n\nAlright, let me write the actual code now!\n\nI'll keep things in three files for good organization.\n\nLet me think through one more edge case: what if we merge branch A into branch B, and then merge branch B into main? The second merge should work correctly because the base of branch B is still the original fork point.\n\nAnd what about merging into a branch that's not the parent? E.g., create feature from main, create feature2 from main, merge feature into feature2. In this case, feature2's base is main at the time feature2 was created. But the common ancestor of feature and feature2 is main. So the LCA isn't necessarily feature2's base...\n\nFor this simple implementation, I'll use the source branch's base (which is the state of its parent at fork time). This works correctly when merging into the parent branch. For other cases, it's not quite correct but it's a reasonable approximation for this exercise.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"find the common ancestor state (the point where the branches diverged)\". For branch B created from branch A, the common ancestor of A and B is A's state at B's creation time. So using B's base IS the correct LCA when merging B into A.\n\nWhen merging A into B (reverse direction), the LCA is still A's state at B's fork time. But B's base is A's state at B's fork time. So it IS the same LCA.\n\nFor criss-cross merges (A -> B, then A changes, B changes, merge A into B again or vice versa), the base doesn't update. The second merge still uses the original base. This is the \"criss-cross merge\" problem, but for this exercise it should be fine.\n\nWait, if I merge B into A and auto-apply, A now has B's changes. But B's base doesn't update. If I then merge B into A again (or A into B), the base is still the original fork point. This means the merge might produce incorrect results because the base is stale.\n\nFor this exercise, I think the simple approach is fine. Handling iterative merges would require updating the base after each merge, which adds complexity.\n\nLet me just code it up now. I'll use the source branch's base.\n\nActually wait, I want to handle one more thing: what if we merge a branch into itself? Like `store.merge('main')` while on 'main'. I'll check for this and treat it as a no-op.\n\nAnd what about merging from a branch that doesn't exist? Throw an error.\n\nOK let me now write the code.\n\n```typescript\n// src/index.ts\n\nimport { deepClone, deepEqual, generateId } from './utils';\nimport { threeWayMerge } from './merge';\nimport { Version, MergeResult, Conflict } from './types';\n\ninterface BranchData {\n  // docId -> current version ID (only for documents that exist on this branch)\n  head: Map<string, string>;\n  // Base state at fork time: docId -> version ID (only for documents that existed at fork)\n  base: Map<string, string>;\n  parent: string | null;\n}\n\nexport class DocumentStore {\n  private versionCounter = 0;\n  private currentBranchName = 'main';\n  \n  // Global stores\n  private versions: Map<string, Version> = new Map();\n  private docVersionList: Map<string, string[]> = new Map();\n  \n  // Branch data\n  private branches: Map<string, BranchData> = new Map();\n  \n  constructor() {\n    this.branches.set('main', {\n      head: new Map(),\n      base: new Map(),\n      parent: null,\n    });\n  }\n  \n  private getBranch(name: string): BranchData {\n    const branch = this.branches.get(name);\n    if (!branch) throw new Error(`Branch \"${name}\" does not exist`);\n    return branch;\n  }\n  \n  private getCurrentBranch(): BranchData {\n    return this.getBranch(this.currentBranchName);\n  }\n  \n  private nextVersionId(): string {\n    return `v${++this.versionCounter}`;\n  }\n  \n  private hasDoc(id: string): boolean {\n    const branch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    return branch.head.has(id);\n  }\n  \n  private getDocVersionList(id: string): string[] {\n    if (!this.docVersionList.has(id)) {\n      this.docVersionList.set(id, []);\n    }\n    return this.docVersionList.get(id)!;\n  }\n  \n  private addVersion(id: string, content: object): string {\n    const versionId = this.nextVersionId();\n    this.versions.set(versionId, {\n      id: versionId,\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n      content: deepClone(content),\n    });\n    this.getDocVersionList(id).push(versionId);\n    return versionId;\n  }\n  \n  // ============== CRUD ==============\n  \n  create(id: string, content: object): void {\n    const branch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    if (branch.head.has(id)) {\n      throw new Error(`Document \"${id}\" already exists on branch \"${this.currentBranchName}\"`);\n    }\n    const versionId = this.addVersion(id, content);\n    branch.head.set(id, versionId);\n  }\n  \n  get(id: string): object {\n    const branch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    const versionId = branch.head.get(id);\n    if (!versionId) {\n      throw new Error(`Document \"${id}\" not found on branch \"${this.currentBranchName}\"`);\n    }\n    const version = this.versions.get(versionId)!;\n    return deepClone(version.content);\n  }\n  \n  update(id: string, content: object): void {\n    const branch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    if (!branch.head.has(id)) {\n      throw new Error(`Document \"${id}\" not found on branch \"${this.currentBranchName}\"`);\n    }\n    const versionId = this.addVersion(id, content);\n    branch.head.set(id, versionId);\n  }\n  \n  delete(id: string): void {\n    const branch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    if (!branch.head.has(id)) {\n      throw new Error(`Document \"${id}\" not found on branch \"${this.currentBranchName}\"`);\n    }\n    branch.head.delete(id);\n  }\n  \n  // ============== History ==============\n  \n  getHistory(id: string): Version[] {\n    const versions = this.getDocVersionList(id);\n    return versions.map(vid => {\n      const v = this.versions.get(vid)!;\n      return { ...v, content: deepClone(v.content) };\n    });\n  }\n  \n  getVersion(id: string, versionId: string): object {\n    const versions = this.getDocVersionList(id);\n    if (!versions.includes(versionId)) {\n      throw new Error(`Version \"${versionId}\" not found for document \"${id}\"`);\n    }\n    const v = this.versions.get(versionId)!;\n    return deepClone(v.content);\n  }\n  \n  // ============== Branching ==============\n  \n  createBranch(name: string, fromBranch?: string): void {\n    if (this.branches.has(name)) {\n      throw new Error(`Branch \"${name}\" already exists`);\n    }\n    const sourceName = fromBranch || this.currentBranchName;\n    const sourceBranch = this.getBranch(sourceName);\n    \n    // Copy current head state\n    const newHead = new Map(sourceBranch.head);\n    \n    // Base state at fork: what existed on the source branch at this point\n    const newBase = new Map(sourceBranch.head);\n    \n    this.branches.set(name, {\n      head: newHead,\n      base: newBase,\n      parent: sourceName,\n    });\n  }\n  \n  switchBranch(name: string): void {\n    if (!this.branches.has(name)) {\n      throw new Error(`Branch \"${name}\" does not exist`);\n    }\n    this.currentBranchName = name;\n  }\n  \n  getBranch(): string {\n    return this.currentBranchName;\n  }\n  \n  listBranches(): string[] {\n    return Array.from(this.branches.keys());\n  }\n  \n  // ============== Merging ==============\n  \n  merge(sourceBranchName: string): MergeResult {\n    if (sourceBranchName === this.currentBranchName) {\n      // Merging branch into itself is a no-op\n      return { merged: {}, conflicts: [], applied: true };\n    }\n    \n    const currentBranch = this.getCurrentBranch();\n    const sourceBranch = this.getBranch(sourceBranchName);\n    const baseState = sourceBranch.base;\n    \n    // Collect all document IDs involved\n    const allDocIds = new Set<string>();\n    for (const id of currentBranch.head.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\n    for (const id of sourceBranch.head.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\n    for (const id of baseState.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\n    \n    const merged: Record<string, object> = {};\n    const conflicts: Conflict[] = [];\n    const appliedUpdates: Map<string, string> = new Map(); // docId -> merged version content\n    \n    for (const docId of allDocIds) {\n      const baseVersionId = baseState.get(docId); // may be undefined\n      const ourVersionId = currentBranch.head.get(docId); // may be undefined\n      const theirVersionId = sourceBranch.head.get(docId); // may be undefined\n      \n      const result = this.mergeDocument(docId, baseVersionId, ourVersionId, theirVersionId, conflicts);\n      \n      if (result !== null) {\n        merged[docId] = result.content;\n        if (result.needsApply) {\n          appliedUpdates.set(docId, result.content);\n        }\n      } else {\n        // Doc doesn't appear in result (both sides deleted it, or it wasn't touched?)\n        // Actually, null means the doc shouldn't appear in merged at all\n      }\n    }\n    \n    if (conflicts.length > 0) {\n      return { merged, conflicts, applied: false };\n    }\n    \n    // Auto-apply the merge\n    for (const [docId, content] of appliedUpdates) {\n      // For existing docs, update them; for new docs, create them\n      // Actually, the content is the merged content, so we create a new version\n      const newVersionId = this.addVersion(docId, content);\n      currentBranch.head.set(docId, newVersionId);\n    }\n    \n    return { merged, conflicts: [], applied: true };\n  }\n  \n  private mergeDocument(\n    docId: string,\n    baseVersionId: string | undefined,\n    ourVersionId: string | undefined,\n    theirVersionId: string | undefined,\n    conflicts: Conflict[],\n  ): { content: object; needsApply: boolean } | null {\n    \n    // Case 1: Doc only exists on our branch (we created it after fork)\n    if (baseVersionId === undefined && ourVersionId !== undefined && theirVersionId === undefined) {\n      return { content: this.getVersionContent(ourVersionId), needsApply: false };\n    }\n    \n    // Case 2: Doc only exists on their branch (they created it after fork)\n    if (baseVersionId === undefined && ourVersionId === undefined && theirVersionId !== undefined) {\n      return { content: this.getVersionContent(theirVersionId), needsApply: true };\n    }\n    \n    // Case 3: Doc exists on both but not in base (both created independently after fork)\n    if (baseVersionId === undefined && ourVersionId !== undefined && theirVersionId !== undefined) {\n      // Three-way merge of two independent creations\n      const base = {};\n      const ours = this.getVersionContent(ourVersionId);\n      const theirs = this.getVersionContent(theirVersionId);\n      const mergedContent = threeWayMerge(base, ours, theirs, docId, '', conflicts);\n      return { content: mergedContent, needsApply: true };\n    }\n    \n    // Case 4: Doc exists in base\n    if (baseVersionId !== undefined) {\n      const base = this.getVersionContent(baseVersionId);\n      \n      // Subcase: Doc deleted on our branch, exists on theirs\n      if (ourVersionId === undefined && theirVersionId !== undefined) {\n        // We deleted, they may or may not have