tinue;\n    } else if (!hasOurs || oVal === undefined) {\n      // We deleted, they kept/modified\n      if (deepEqual(bVal, tVal)) {\n        // They didn't change -> fine, take deletion\n        continue;\n      } else {\n        // They modified -> conflict\n        conflicts.push({\n          documentId: docId,\n          path: childPath,\n          base: deepClone(bVal),\n          ours: undefined,\n          theirs: deepClone(tVal),\n        });\n        // Favor ours (deletion) -> skip key\n        continue;\n      }\n    } else if (!hasTheirs || tVal === undefined) {\n      // They deleted, we kept/modified\n      if (deepEqual(bVal, oVal)) {\n        // We didn't change -> take deletion\n        continue;\n      } else {\n        // We modified -> conflict\n        conflicts.push({\n          documentId: docId,\n          path: childPath,\n          base: deepClone(bVal),\n          ours: deepClone(oVal),\n          theirs: undefined,\n        });\n        // Favor ours (keep our value)\n        result[key] = deepClone(oVal);\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n    // Key exists in all three\n    else {\n      result[key] = threeWayMerge(bVal, oVal, tVal, docId, childPath, conflicts);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, there's an issue. When both sides deleted a key that existed in base, we should skip it. But with the condition `(!hasOurs || oVal === undefined) && (!hasTheirs || tVal === undefined)`, this would catch `!hasOurs && !hasTheirs` (both deleted by omission - i.e., key removed from object) AND `hasOurs && oVal === undefined && hasTheirs && tVal === undefined` (both explicitly set to undefined - which can't happen in JSON since undefined isn't a valid value, but in JS objects).\n\nActually in JSON objects, `JSON.stringify({a: undefined, b: 1})` just removes `a`. So `a in obj` is false if `a` was set to undefined. So `!hasOurs && !hasTheirs` is the correct way to check both deleted.\n\nBut what about the case where `hasOurs` is 