the LCA of A and B is:\n- If A == B: A\n- If A is an ancestor of B: A\n- If B is an ancestor of A: B\n- Otherwise, recurse to (parent(A), B) or (A, parent(B))\n\nAnd the base state for the merge is the STATE of the LCA branch when the later-branching child diverged from it.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me just use a very simple heuristic that covers the main use cases:\n\n```typescript\nprivate getMergeBase(sourceBranch: string, currentBranch: BranchData, sourceBranchData: BranchData): Map<string, string> {\n  // Walk up from source's parent chain to find if current is an ancestor\n  let parent = sourceBranchData.parent;\n  while (parent) {\n    if (parent === this.currentBranchName) {\n      // Source branched from current -> use source's base\n      return sourceBranchData.base;\n    }\n    const pBranch = this.branches.get(parent);\n    parent = pBranch ? pBranch.parent : null;\n  }\n  \n  // Check if current branched from source\n  parent = currentBranch.parent;\n  while (parent) {\n    if (parent === sourceBranch) {\n      // Current branched from source -> use current's base\n      return currentBranch.base;\n    }\n    const pBranch = this.branches.get(parent);\n    parent = pBranch ? pBranch.parent : null;\n  }\n  \n  // Otherwise, find LCA by walking both parent chains\n  // and use the LCA's state\n  // ... for now, fall back to source's base\n  return sourceBranchData.base;\n}\n```\n\nActually, I'm overthinking this. For a simple versioned document store, the most common merge pattern is merging a feature branch into main (its parent). For that case, the source's base is correct.\n\nLet me just use the source's base and document that limitation. If the tests require the more general case, I can extend it.\n\nActually, you know what, I just realized there might be a simpler way to think about all of this. Instead of tracking per-branch bases, I can store the \"fork point\" for each branch. When branch B is created from branch A, I record:\n- B's parent = A\n- B's forkState = deep copy of A's current state\n\nThen for any merge, I walk the parent chain to find the LCA. The LCA is the deepest (closest to the two branches) node that is an ancestor of both. And the base state is that branch's state at the time the two diverged.\n\nBut the LCA branch might have changed since the divergence. So the base state isn't the LCA's current state, but the LCA's state at the time each child diverged.\n\nThis is hard because two children of the same parent might diverge at different times, and the parent might change between them.\n\nYou know what, let me just go with a simpler model that assumes:\n\n1. Branches are created from the current (or specified) branch\n2. Each branch stores its base state (the state of its parent at fork time)\n3. Merging always uses the SOURCE branch's base\n\nThis works correctly for:\n- Merging child into parent (feature -> main) ✓\n- Merging sibling into sibling (feature2 -> feature1) — kinda works, base is correct for feature2 but feature1 might have diverged from main too\n\nFor sibling-to-sibling merges, using the source's base (which is main's state when source was created) as the common ancestor is a reasonable approximation. The three-way merge will compare:\n- base: main at source's creation\n- ours: current state of destination sibling\n- theirs: current state of source sibling\n\nThis is not quite correct because the destination sibling also diverged from main, but the three-way merge will handle simple cases well. If both siblings modified different fields, the merge will succeed.\n\nFor this exercise, I think this is sufficient. Let me go with it.\n\nLet me also think about what happens when we merge and then need to update the base. If I merge feature into main (auto-apply), main now has all of feature's changes. But feature's base is still the original fork point. If I continue working on feature and merge again, it will use the same base and re-detect the same changes.\n\nActually