n\nFor the case where both sides have the same B changes (from the first merge):\n- base: value V\n- ours: value V' (from first merge)\n- theirs: value V' (from original feature)\n\nSince ours == theirs (V' == V'), no conflict.\n\nFor new changes on feature after first merge:\n- base: value V\n- ours: value V' (first merge)\n- theirs: value V'' (feature after second change)\n\nSince ours != base (V' != V), theirs != base (V'' != V), ours != theirs (V' != V''), and ours matches neither base nor theirs -> conflict!\n\nBut this SHOULDN'T be a conflict! The user made new changes on feature, and we're merging them into main. Feature's changes should just be taken since we're merging feature INTO main.\n\nHmm wait, in this scenario:\n- main (ours) has V' from the first merge\n- feature (theirs) has V'' which is a NEW change on top of V'\n\nV'' was made on feature AFTER the first merge. Merging feature into main again:\n- base: V (original)\n- ours: V' (first merge result on main)\n- theirs: V'' (new change on feature)\n\nThree-way merge sees: base=V, ours=V', theirs=V''\n- ours != base: main changed V to V'\n- theirs != base: feature changed V to V''\n- ours != theirs: different values\n\nThis IS a conflict, but in reality it's not. The correct behavior should be: since V'' is a NEW change on feature that builds on top of V', and V' was already in main from the first merge, V'' should be applied cleanly.\n\nBut our three-way merge doesn't know about the sequence of changes. It just sees three versions: V, V', V'' where all three are different -> conflict.\n\nThis is a fundamental limitation of a three-way merge. To handle this correctly, you'd need recursive merge or knowledge of the change history. For this exercise, I think this is fine - the basic three-way merge with the fork point as base is what's expected.\n\nOK, I'm going to stop overthinking this and just write the code. Let me close my analysis and start implementation.\n\nLet me now think about testing. I'll al