rceBranchName)!.base;\n  }\n  \n  // If current is the LCA, source is a descendant -> use source's base\n  if (lca === currentBranchName) {\n    return this.branches.get(sourceBranchName)!.base;\n  }\n  \n  // If source is the LCA, current is a descendant -> use current's base\n  if (lca === sourceBranchName) {\n    return this.branches.get(currentBranchName)!.base;\n  }\n  \n  // Both have forked from LCA at different points\n  // Walk from LCA to source to find the direct child of LCA\n  let current = sourceBranchName;\n  while (current) {\n    const branch = this.branches.get(current)!;\n    if (branch.parent === lca) {\n      return branch.base;\n    }\n    current = branch.parent!;\n  }\n  \n  // Fallback: use source's base\n  return this.branches.get(sourceBranchName)!.base;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this still might not be correct if we merge from a branch that was created from a branch that was created from main, and the intermediate branch has been updated since. The fork point should be the LCA's state at the time the intermediate branch was created, not the current state.\n\nLet me simplify even further and just use the source branch's base for all merges. This is the simplest approach that works for the common case (merging a branch into its parent), and is a reasonable approximation for other cases.\n\nFor the scope of this exercise, I'll use this approach:\n- Use the source branch's base for the merge base\n- This works correctly when source branch was created from current branch\n- It's a reasonable approximation for other cases\n\nOK let me code this up.\n\nActually, wait - I want to handle the case where `currentBranchName` is a child of `sourceBranchName` correctly. If I'm on `feature` and merge `main` into it:\n- Source is `main`, source's base is `{}` (empty, since main is root)\n- But the LCA is actually main's state when feature was created, which is feature's base\n- Using source's (main's) base would give us an empty base, which would compare feature's changes against an empty object -> lots of \"conflicts\"\n\nSo I really should handle this case. Let me use this logic:\n\n```\ngetMergeBase(current, source):\n  if source is descendant of current:\n    use source's base\n  elif current is descendant of source:\n    use current's base\n  else:\n    find LCA of both\n    walk from LCA to source, find first direct child of LCA, use that child's base\n```\n\nActually, for the case where both are descendants of LCA (sibling branches), the base should be the LCA's state. But which branch's base do we use? The first one that forked from the LCA.\n\nHmm, actually if they're siblings (both forked from main), and we merge feature2 into feature1:\n- LCA = main\n- feature1 forked from main at time T1\n- feature2 forked from main at time T2\n- The base should be main's state at min(T1, T2)... no, actually it should be main's state at max(T1, T2)? \n\nNo wait. The three-way merge compares three states. The base should be the LAST common state before divergence. If feature1 forked at T1 and feature2 forked at T2 (T2 > T1), and main changed between T1 and T2:\n- feature1 base = main at T1\n- feature2 base = main at T2\n- feature1 might not have the changes main made between T1 and T2\n- feature2 might have those changes\n\nThe common ancestor for feature1 and feature2 should be main at T1 (the earlier fork time) because feature1 diverged from main at T1 and feature2 diverged from main at T2, but feature1 never had main's T1->T2 changes.\n\nSo the base should be the EARLIER fork point's state. But we don't know when each branch forked (we didn't store the timestamp).\n\nFor simplicity, I'll use the source branch's base when merging source into current, and handle the case where current is a descendant of source separately.\n\nOK let me just do the simplest approach and test it:\n\n```\ngetMergeBase(current, source):\n  if current.branches.get(source)!.parent === currentBranchName:\n    // Source is direct child of current -> u